The C1-C2 black group begins with exactly two liberties (B1, B2). White's move at B2 places that group in atari. Black's only legal reply is B1 (any other move lets White capture immediately at B1). After Black plays B1 the resulting three-stone chain has only one remaining liberty (A1). White then plays A1, which is legal because it fills the last liberty and removes the three black stones.  

If White begins at B1 instead, Black can answer at B2, linking A2-B3-B4-C2-C1 into a single chain whose liberties are no longer forced to zero; therefore B1 does not guarantee capture against all replies. No other empty intersection initiates a forcing sequence that captures any black group regardless of Black's subsequent play. Hence the sole move that satisfies the problem condition is B2.

